<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=thermometer">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thermometer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1625">1625</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A thermometer measures temperature through a change in a sensor that is read against a scale. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byzantium">Philo of Byzantium</a> in the 3rd century BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hero-of-alexandria">Hero of Alexandria</a> described devices in which heated and cooled air moved water, and in the late 16th and early 17th centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> and Santorio Santorio built thermoscopes, which had no scale. The first instrument shown with a scale, and so a thermometer, was Santorio's of 1625; in 1629 Joseph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Delmedigo described a sealed liquid-in-glass thermometer. Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit made a reliable mercury thermometer in 1714, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anders-celsius">Anders Celsius</a> proposed his scale in 1742.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thermometer.jpg" alt="Thermometer" width="330" height="712" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonimski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mercury_Thermometer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1625</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Santorio Santorio</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Galileo is often said to be the inventor, but no surviving document shows that he made such an instrument. Jean Leurechon described one with a scale of 8 degrees in 1624.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=thermometer">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
